  "function_translated_nl": "Lesson context: lesson_05. Let triangle ABC be a triangle with sides AB = 14, BC = 16, AC = 10. Let triangle MNP be a triangle with sides NP = 7, MP = 5. Let segment MN be a midsegment segment. Let segment NP be a midsegment segment with length 7. Let segment MP be a midsegment segment with length 5. ABC is dilated to form MNP with scale factor 0.5, centered at point A. Segment MN is parallel to BC. Segment NP is parallel to AC. Segment MP is parallel to AB. Find the length of MN.",
  "student_facing_prose": "A contractor is designing a support structure for a roof truss. She sketches triangle ABC with sides AB = 14 inches, BC = 16 inches, and AC = 10 inches. To reinforce the frame, she plans to add internal bracing by connecting two points M and N on the sides of the triangle, where M lies on side AB and N lies on side AC, each positioned at the midpoint of their respective sides.\n\nBecause of how the bracing is positioned, the smaller triangle MNP formed by these midpoints and a third point P will be a dilation of the original triangle ABC, centered at vertex A with a scale factor of 0.5.\n\nThis means:\n- Segment MN will be parallel to side BC\n- Segment NP will be parallel to side AC, with length NP = 7 inches\n- Segment MP will be parallel to side AB, with length MP = 5 inches\n\nTo complete her design plans, the contractor needs to know the length of the internal brace segment MN. What should she calculate?"
}